\section{Checking a Supplied Inductive Invariant}

A candidate invariant can come from widening, another tool, or a hand-written assertion. Its origin is irrelevant to a checker: what matters is whether it contains the initial states and is closed under every transition. This separates finding an approximation, which may be expensive or heuristic, from validating it.

\textbf{Inputs and assumptions.} Given a map $I$ from control points to abstract states, use a sound entry abstraction $S$ and sound edge transformers $T_e^\sharp$. These include guard filtering on the appropriate edges. At each assertion point $v$, the checker needs a bound $P_v$ whose concretization contains only states that satisfy the assertion. A missing invariant entry is an error, not an implicit proof that the point is unreachable.

\begin{sudocode}[Validate local proof obligations]
\Require Entry $s$, candidate $I$, assertion bounds $P$
\Ensure Acceptance implies all assertions hold
\If{$I$ is incomplete}
  \State \Return malformed candidate
\EndIf
\If{$S\not\sqsubseteq I(s)$}
  \State \Return entry obligation \Comment{Include zero-step runs}
\EndIf
\ForAll{edges $e:u\to v$}
  \If{$T_e^\sharp(I(u))\not\sqsubseteq I(v)$}
    \State \Return edge $e$ \Comment{Closure, not equality}
  \EndIf
\EndFor
\ForAll{assertion points $v$}
  \If{$I(v)\not\sqsubseteq P_v$}
    \State \Return assertion at $v$
  \EndIf
\EndFor
\State \Return accepted
\end{sudocode}

\textbf{Example.} Let a loop enter with $x=0$, add 2 when $x<5$, and exit otherwise. The interval $[0,5]$ at the head fails: filtering to $[0,4]$ and adding 2 yields $[2,6]$. With $[0,6]$ at the head and $[5,6]$ at the exit, the entry and both edges pass. An exit assertion $x\leq 6$ is therefore certified. The presence of the unreachable value 5 in the exit interval is harmless for this assertion.

\textbf{Invariant.} By induction on the number of executed edges, every reachable state belongs to the candidate at its control point. Acceptance does not show that $I$ is the least solution. Rejection, even of the assertion check, is not a concrete counterexample. The checker makes one transfer and one inclusion test per edge, plus inclusion tests for entry and assertions; their individual cost depends on the chosen domain.

\section{Summary}\label{sec:ai-theory-summary}

Abstract interpretation provides the mathematical foundation for sound program analysis. The key concepts are:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Concrete vs.\ abstract semantics}: The concrete semantics describes actual behavior; the abstract semantics over-approximates it.
  \item \textbf{Galois connections}: The formal relationship between concrete and abstract domains.
  \item \textbf{Soundness}: The abstract analysis never misses a concrete behavior.
  \item \textbf{Completeness}: The abstract analysis loses no precision beyond what is inherent in the abstraction.
  \item \textbf{Best correct approximation}: The most precise sound abstract function.
  \item \textbf{Domain refinement}: Systematic methods for improving precision through Moore closures and completion.
  \item \textbf{Reduced product}: Combining domains with information exchange for improved precision.
\end{itemize}
These concepts provide a principled framework for designing, implementing, and reasoning about program analyses, and they form the foundation for the more specific techniques developed in the following chapters. Abstract interpretation has been applied to termination analysis, security verification, and many other program properties beyond the classical dataflow analyses.

\section{Galois Connections: Formal Treatment}\label{sec:galois-formal}

A \emph{Galois connection} is the fundamental mathematical structure linking concrete and abstract domains. We present a thorough treatment here.

\begin{definition}[Galois Connection]\label{def:galois-connection}
Let $(C, \sqsubseteq_C)$ and $(A, \sqsubseteq_A)$ be complete lattices. A \textbf{Galois connection} between $C$ and $A$ is a pair of functions $\alpha : C \to A$ (abstraction) and $\gamma : A \to C$ (concretization) such that:
\[
\forall c \in C, a \in A: \quad \alpha(c) \sqsubseteq_A a \iff c \sqsubseteq_C \gamma(a)
\]
We write this as $(C, \sqsubseteq_C, \alpha, \gamma, A, \sqsubseteq_A)$ or simply $\langle \alpha, \gamma \rangle : C \rightleftarrows A$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Properties of Galois Connections]\label{thm:galois-props}
If $\langle \alpha, \gamma \rangle$ is a Galois connection, then:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item $\alpha$ and $\gamma$ are monotone: $c_1 \sqsubseteq c_2 \implies \alpha(c_1) \sqsubseteq \alpha(c_2)$
  \item $\alpha \circ \gamma \sqsubseteq \mathrm{id}_A$ (reduction: $\alpha(\gamma(a)) \sqsubseteq a$)
  \item $\mathrm{id}_C \sqsubseteq \gamma \circ \alpha$ (extensivity: $c \sqsubseteq \gamma(\alpha(c))$)
  \item $\alpha \circ \gamma \circ \alpha = \alpha$ and $\gamma \circ \alpha \circ \gamma = \gamma$
  \item $\alpha$ preserves all suprema: $\alpha(\bigsqcup S) = \bigsqcup \alpha(S)$
  \item $\gamma$ preserves all infima: $\gamma(\bigsqcap S) = \bigsqcap \gamma(S)$
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Galois Insertions}\label{sec:galois-insertion}

\begin{definition}[Galois Insertion]\label{def:galois-insertion}
A Galois connection $\langle \alpha, \gamma \rangle$ is a \textbf{Galois insertion} if $\alpha \circ \gamma = \mathrm{id}_A$, i.e., $\alpha(\gamma(a)) = a$ for all $a \in A$. This means the abstraction is \emph{exact}: no information is lost when going from abstract to concrete and back.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Galois Insertion Characterization]\label{thm:galois-insertion-char}
A Galois connection is a Galois insertion if and only if $\alpha$ is surjective (onto). Equivalently, every element of $A$ is the abstraction of some concrete element.
\end{theorem}

\begin{example}[Galois Insertion: Sign Domain]\label{ex:sign-insertion}
The sign domain $\{\bot, -, 0, +, \top\}$ forms a Galois insertion with $\wp(\mathbb{Z})$:
\begin{verbatim}
  α({-3, -1}) = -      γ(-) = {..., -3, -2, -1}
  α({0, 5}) = +        γ(+) = {1, 2, 3, ...}
  α(∅) = ⊥             γ(⊥) = ∅
  α(ℤ) = ⊤             γ(⊤) = ℤ
\end{verbatim}
Here $\alpha(\gamma(+)) = \alpha(\{1, 2, 3, \ldots\}) = +$, so it is a Galois insertion.
\end{example}

\subsection{Constructing Galois Connections}\label{sec:constructing-gc}

Given a concretization function $\gamma$, the corresponding abstraction function (if it exists) is uniquely determined:

\begin{definition}[Best Abstraction]\label{def:best-abstraction}
Given $\gamma : A \to C$, the \textbf{best abstraction} is:
\[
\alpha(c) = \bigsqcap_A \{a \in A \mid c \sqsubseteq_C \gamma(a)\}
\]
This exists when $A$ is a complete lattice and $\gamma$ preserves all infima.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Galois Connection from $\gamma$]\label{thm:gc-from-gamma}
If $\gamma : A \to C$ preserves all infima, then $\langle \alpha, \gamma \rangle$ is a Galois connection where $\alpha(c) = \bigsqcap \{a \mid c \sqsubseteq \gamma(a)\}$.
\end{theorem}

\section{Completeness in Abstract Interpretation}\label{sec:completeness-detail}

\emph{Completeness} means that the abstract analysis loses no precision beyond what is inherent in the abstraction itself.

\begin{definition}[Abstract Domain Completeness]\label{def:domain-completeness}
An abstract domain $A$ is \textbf{complete} for a concrete operation $f : C \to C$ if:
\[
\alpha(f(\gamma(a))) = f_A(a) \quad \text{for all } a \in A
\]
That is, the abstract operation $f_A$ is as precise as possible given the abstraction.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Transfer Function Completeness]\label{def:tf-completeness}
A transfer function $f_A$ is \textbf{complete} for $f$ if:
\[
\alpha(f(\gamma(a))) = f_A(a) \quad \text{for all } a \in A
\]
This means no precision is lost in the abstract computation beyond the abstraction of the inputs.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Completeness of Interval Domain]\label{ex:interval-completeness}
Consider $f(x) = x + 1$ on the interval domain:
\begin{verbatim}
  f_A([a, b]) = [a + 1, b + 1]
  α(f(γ([a, b]))) = α({x + 1 | x ∈ [a, b]}) = [a + 1, b + 1]
\end{verbatim}
The interval addition is complete for $f(x) = x + 1$. However, for $f(x) = x * x$:
\begin{verbatim}
  f_A([-1, 1]) = [-1, 1]  (naive interval multiplication)
  α(f(γ([-1, 1]))) = α({x² | x ∈ [-1, 1]}) = [0, 1]
\end{verbatim}
The naive interval multiplication is \emph{incomplete}: it loses the information that the result is non-negative.
\end{example}

\subsection{Completeness and Domain Refinement}\label{sec:completeness-refinement}

\begin{theorem}[Completeness by Refinement]\label{thm:completeness-refine}
For any abstract domain $A$ and concrete operation $f$, there exists a most abstract domain $A'$ that is complete for $f$ and at least as precise as $A$. This domain is obtained by \emph{completing} $A$ with respect to $f$.
\end{theorem}

The completion of $A$ with respect to $f$ is:
\[
A^f = \mathcal{M}(\alpha(f(\gamma(A))))
\]
where $\mathcal{M}$ is the Moore closure operator that makes the set closed under arbitrary intersections.

\section{Fixpoint Precision and Abstract Fixpoints}\label{sec:fixpoint-precision}

The precision of the computed fixpoint depends on both the abstract domain and the iteration strategy.

\begin{definition}[Abstract Fixpoint]\label{def:abstract-fixpoint}
The \textbf{abstract fixpoint} $\mathit{lfp}_A(F_A)$ is the least fixedpoint of the abstract transfer function $F_A$ on the abstract domain $A$. By soundness:
\[
\mathit{lfp}_A(F_A) \sqsupseteq \alpha(\mathit{lfp}_C(F_C))
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Fixpoint Precision]\label{thm:fixpoint-precision}
If the abstract domain is complete for $F_C$ (the concrete transfer function), then the abstract fixpoint is exact:
\[
\mathit{lfp}_A(F_A) = \alpha(\mathit{lfp}_C(F_C))
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{definition}[Least Collecting Semantics]\label{def:least-collecting}
The \textbf{least collecting semantics} is the most precise concrete semantics that tracks the set of reachable states at each program point. The abstract interpretation of the least collecting semantics gives the most precise analysis result for a given abstract domain.
\end{definition}

\section{Relational vs.\ Non-Relational Abstractions}\label{sec:relational-vs-nonrel}

\begin{definition}[Non-Relational Abstract Domain]\label{def:non-relational}
An abstract domain is \textbf{non-relational} (or \emph{independent-of-variables}) if it tracks properties of individual variables independently. Formally, $A = A_1 \times A_2 \times \cdots \times A_n$ where each $A_i$ abstracts a single variable.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Relational Abstract Domain]\label{def:relational-domain}
An abstract domain is \textbf{relational} if it can express relationships between variables. Examples include the polyhedra domain ($x + y \leq 10$), the octagon domain ($|x - y| \leq 5$), and the congruence domain ($x \equiv y \pmod{3}$).
\end{definition}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\hline
\textbf{Property} & \textbf{Non-Relational} & \textbf{Relational} \\
\hline
Expressiveness & Individual variable properties & Variable relationships \\
Cost per operation & $O(n)$ & $O(n^2)$ to $O(n^3)$ \\
Example & Interval, Sign & Polyhedra, Octagon \\
Precision for & Simple bounds & Array indices, loop invariants \\
Scalability & High (thousands of variables) & Limited (hundreds of variables) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Comparison of relational and non-relational abstract domains.}
\label{tab:rel-vs-nonrel}
\end{table}

\section{Abstract Interpretation of Concurrency}\label{sec:ai-concurrency}

Abstract interpretation has been extended to concurrent programs, where the interleaving of threads creates an exponential state space.

\begin{definition}[Thread-Modular Abstract Interpretation]\label{def:thread-modular}
\textbf{Thread-modular} abstract interpretation analyzes each thread separately, using an \emph{interference relation} to summarize the effect of other threads. The interference relation is computed iteratively:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Start with a conservative interference relation (e.g., all shared variables may be modified arbitrarily).
  \item Analyze each thread under the current interference relation.
  \item Update the interference relation based on the analysis results.
  \item Repeat until a fixpoint is reached.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Thread-Modular Soundness]\label{thm:thread-modular-sound}
Thread-modular abstract interpretation is sound: the computed invariants hold for all possible interleavings of the threads.
\end{theorem}

\begin{definition}[Rely-Guarantee Abstract Interpretation]\label{def:rely-guarantee}
\textbf{Rely-guarantee} abstract interpretation extends thread-modular analysis by associating each thread with:
\begin{itemize}
  \item A \emph{rely} condition: an over-approximation of the interference from other threads.
  \item A \emph{guarantee} condition: an over-approximation of the interference this thread causes to others.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}
